course: biology
lesson: 261
title: The human body deeper: the heart and blood in amazing numbers
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Your heart is a muscle about the size of your own fist, and it never takes a day off: it squeezes blood out with every beat, more than 100,000 times a day.
explain: Each squeeze sends a wave along your arteries, and that wave is the pulse you can feel in your wrist after a race in the playground.
explain: The blood travels through tubes called blood vessels, so many of them that laid end to end they would go more than twice around the Earth, and a drop sets off round the whole body and is back in about a minute.
explain: Blood is never blue: it is bright red when it is full of oxygen and darker red when it has given some away; veins only look blue because of the way skin lets light through.
explain: Cartoons like Tom and Jerry show a heart bursting out of Tom's chest when he falls in love, but a real heart stays safely inside the ribs, beating faster when you run, get scared or excited; in a race it speeds up to send more blood to the working muscles.
fact: Each heartbeat squeezes blood out and sends a wave along the arteries, which you feel as your pulse.
fact: A child's heart beats about 70 to 110 times a minute at rest, which is more than 100,000 times a day.
fact: Your heart is about the size of your own fist and has four rooms called chambers; the «lub-dub» sound is its valves snapping shut.
fact: An adult's blood vessels laid end to end would stretch roughly 100,000 kilometres, more than twice around the Earth; this is an estimate, and books give different numbers.
fact: Blood is always red, bright when it carries plenty of oxygen and darker when it carries less; veins look blue only because of how light passes through skin.
fact: Blood has red cells that carry oxygen, white cells that fight germs, and platelets that help stop bleeding; one tiny drop holds millions of red cells.
fact: When you cut yourself, platelets and stringy fibres make a net that plugs the cut and hardens into a scab.
fact: An adult has about 5 litres of blood, and one blood donation is about half a litre, which the body soon makes again.
fact: The blood groups A, B, AB and O were discovered by Karl Landsteiner in 1901, which made giving blood from one person to another much safer, because some blood groups do not mix safely and before that nobody knew which.
fact: Red blood cells are made in the bone marrow, the soft middle of big bones, about 2 million every second.
fact: The stethoscope was invented in 1816 by René Laennec, who first used a rolled-up tube of paper to listen to a heart; before that, doctors put an ear straight onto the patient's chest.
fact: A blue whale's heart weighs about 180 kilograms and can beat as slowly as two times a minute on a deep dive; small animals have fast hearts and big animals slow ones.
fact: When you exercise, blood vessels in your skin widen to let heat out, so your face goes red; regular exercise makes the heart stronger, so it beats more slowly at rest.
exercise: What makes the pulse you feel in your wrist? | A wave in the arteries from each squeeze of the heart | Air moving along inside the veins of your arm with every breath | The bones of your wrist clicking | Your muscles twitching on their own
exercise: About how many times does a child's heart beat in a day? | More than 100,000 times | About 1,000 times | Exactly 70 times | About 10 million times
exercise: About how big is your heart? | About the size of your own fist | About the size of a football | About the size of a pea | About the size of your whole chest
exercise: How many chambers, or rooms, does a human heart have? | Four | Two | One | Six
exercise: What makes the «lub-dub» sound of the heart? | Its valves snapping shut | Blood hitting the ribs | Air bubbles bursting in it | The heart rubbing on the lungs
exercise: Roughly how long would all of an adult's blood vessels be, laid end to end? | About 100,000 kilometres, more than twice round the Earth | About 100 metres in all, roughly the length of a football pitch | About 1 kilometre, a short walk | About 10 kilometres, a long run
exercise: What colour is the blood inside your veins? | Dark red | Blue | Purple | Green
exercise: Which part of the blood carries oxygen around the body? | Red blood cells | White blood cells | Platelets | Plasma bubbles
exercise: What do white blood cells do? | Fight germs | Carry oxygen | Make scabs on their own | Turn food into blood
exercise: How does a scab form on a cut? | Platelets and stringy fibres make a net that plugs the cut and hardens | Skin melts over the cut and goes hard | Dust from the air sticks to the wet cut and dries into a lid over the hole | White cells turn into hard skin
exercise: About how much blood does an adult have? | About 5 litres | About half a litre | About 50 litres | About one cupful
exercise: Who discovered the blood groups A, B, AB and O? | Karl Landsteiner | René Laennec | Charles Darwin | Louis Pasteur
exercise: Where are red blood cells made? | In the bone marrow | In the heart's chambers | In the stomach lining | In the lungs
exercise: What did René Laennec use for the very first stethoscope? | A rolled-up tube of paper | A metal trumpet with a wide end | A hollow bamboo cane | A glass bottle
exercise: Which animal has a heart weighing about 180 kilograms? | The blue whale | The elephant | The giraffe | The hippopotamus
exercise: Why does your face go red in PE? | Blood vessels in the skin widen to let heat out | Your blood turns brighter red when you run | Your heart squeezes blood into your cheeks to save it | Your skin gets thinner when you move
exercise: What does regular exercise do to your resting heartbeat? | It makes it slower, because the heart gets stronger | It makes it faster for ever, even when you sleep | It stops it for a few seconds | It makes no difference at all
test: «my sister says blood is blue till it hits the air, look at my veins, they're blue innit» What is right? | No, blood is always red; veins only look blue through the skin | Yes, blood is blue inside you and turns red the moment that air touches it | Yes, but only in your arms | No, blood is green inside and red outside
test: In Tom and Jerry, Tom's heart bursts out of his chest and thumps in the air when he sees a pretty cat. What really happens when you are excited? | The heart beats faster, but it stays safely inside the ribs | The heart really does jump a little way up out of the chest, then drops back | The heart stops until you calm down | The heart swells to twice its size
test: Count them: 3 children each give blood twice a year, half a litre each time. How much blood is given in a year? | 3 litres | 1.5 litres | 6 litres | 2 litres
test: «a mouse heart vs an elephant heart, which one beats faster, go» | The mouse's, because small animals have fast hearts | The elephant's, because big hearts are stronger and beat harder | They beat exactly the same | Neither, mice have no heart
test: What would happen if your blood had no platelets? | A small cut would keep bleeding and not make a scab | Your blood would turn blue | You would not be able to feel your pulse in your wrist or your neck | Your bones would stop making blood
test: «mum gave blood today, she's got loads less now, will she run out??» What is best? | No, half a litre is a small part of her 5 litres and her body makes it again | Yes, she has given away almost all of the blood she had | Yes, blood never grows back in grown-ups, so it is gone for ever | No, because she gave only white blood cells
test: Count them: a doctor listens to 2 elephants with a giant stethoscope. How many trunks, tails and hearts are there in all? | 2 trunks, 2 tails and 2 hearts | 2 trunks, 2 tails and 4 hearts | 4 trunks, 2 tails and 2 hearts | 2 trunks, 4 tails and 2 hearts
test: Before 1816, how did a doctor usually listen to a patient's heart? | By putting an ear straight onto the chest | By looking into the mouth with a lamp | By squeezing the patient's wrist very hard and counting | By tapping the head with a hammer
test: «bone marrow's just fat in bones, it's for dogs to eat lol» What is right? | Red bone marrow makes your blood cells, millions every second | Right, marrow does nothing for the body, it is just soft filling | Marrow makes your bones grow longer only, nothing else at all | Marrow is where the body keeps its air
test: A blue whale dives deep and stays down a long time. What happens to its heartbeat? | It slows down, to as little as two beats a minute | It speeds up to a thousand beats a minute | It stops completely until the whale comes up | It beats exactly like a child's heart
test: Before blood groups were known, giving one person's blood to another often went badly. Why? | Some blood groups do not mix safely, and nobody knew which | Blood always went bad as soon as it left the body | Doctors used animal blood, from sheep and calves, instead of human blood | People got blood that was too cold
test: «ran round the field twice, my heart's going mental, is it broken» What is most likely? | No, a heart beats faster in exercise to send more blood to the muscles | Yes, a heart should beat the same all the time, whatever you are doing | Yes, running damages the heart every time | No, it is your lungs beating, not your heart
exam: Identify the principal function of the red blood cells within the human circulatory system. | The conveyance of oxygen to the tissues of the body | The destruction of invading micro-organisms | The formation of scabs upon wounds | The production of the heart's audible sounds
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the colour of human blood within the veins. | It is red of a darker shade, the appearance of blueness arising from the passage of light through the skin | It is blue, becoming red only upon exposure to the atmosphere | It is colourless until it reaches the heart | It is purple in children and red in adults
exam: Ascertain the anatomical site in which red blood cells are principally produced. | The marrow within the bones | The chambers of the heart | The lining of the stomach | The air sacs of the lungs
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between the size of an animal and the rate of its heartbeat? | Smaller animals generally possess faster heartbeats, and larger animals slower ones | Larger animals invariably possess faster heartbeats, their hearts being larger and stronger | All animals possess an identical rate of heartbeat | The rate of heartbeat is determined solely by colour
